"Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has announced the public preview of 'Mistral Large 4', its next-generation open-weight multimodal model featuring 1 trillion total parameters and 52 billion active parameters. Mistral Large 4 is a native multimodal model with 52 billion active parameters out of 1 trillion total parameters, delivering state-of-the-art performance across coding, agentic workflows, cybersecurity, and enterprise workloads. Trained from scratch using 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs in its proprietary European data centers, it was trained on multilingual data spanning more than 160 languages, including all official EU languages. Currently available as a preview API via Mistral Studio, open weights are scheduled to be released later this month following red-teaming evaluations with partners and security authorities. Introducing Timestral Mistral AI has unveiled 'Timestral,' a family of multivariate time-series foundation models with sizes up to 209M parameters that ranked first across major benchmarks. Timestral is a time-series foundation model available in four sizes—5M, 28M, 123M, and 209M parameters—that can run efficiently even on CPUs. Combining synthetic data pre-training, covariate-aware attention, and long-context training, it supports past and future covariates and demonstrates outstanding zero-shot forecasting performance. The largest 209M model achieved first place on major time-series benchmarks including GIFT-Eval, TIME, and fev-bench, using only two-thirds the parameters of prior state-of-the-art models. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral establishes a new hub in Munich, Germany, partnering with manufacturing companies in Germany—Europe's largest industrial powerhouse—to accelerate the development of industrial and physics-based AI (Physics AI). Mistral opens its Munich hub and deploys dedicated research teams and engineers for Physics AI and industrial AI to build long-term technology partnerships with German companies. Advancing the development of physical simulation AI for European heavy industry, including crash simulation and engineering AI collaboration with BMW, and industrial AI applications with Siemens Energy. Building 1 GW of European computing capacity by 2030 to secure European AI sovereignty, and supporting on-premises deployment based on open-weight architectures. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla have formed a partnership to integrate open-source, privacy-focused multilingual AI models into Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Smart Window'. Mistral AI models are applied to the beta version of Mozilla's Firefox AI browsing assistant, 'Smart Window'. It provides features such as organizing complex searches, remembering previously viewed information, and identifying sources based on open tabs, with plans to expand service starting from France and North America to the United Kingdom and Germany. It supports fine-tuning tailored to regional languages, dialects, and cultural contexts, as well as robust privacy protection through a 'zero data retention' policy on Mozilla servers and from Mistral. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral and Cloudera have formed a partnership to enable enterprises to build and operate customized AI models while maintaining their data sovereignty and security. Integrates Mistral's models with Cloudera's hybrid data platform to securely run AI inference across on-premises, public and private clouds, and fully isolated (air-gapped) environments. Enables enterprises in regulated industries (finance, manufacturing, telecommunications, etc.) to leverage massive proprietary datasets to train and build business-specific AI models while retaining full data ownership. Brings Sovereign AI capabilities—giving customers direct control over data, intelligence, compute, and operations—to the massive scale of data managed by the Cloudera platform. Mark Wahlberg on using AI in film ‘the right way’ and being open to an AI clone carrying on his legacy Actor Mark Wahlberg expressed a positive stance on utilizing AI appropriately in filmmaking and the potential use of an AI clone to preserve his legacy posthumously. On stage at TechCrunch Disrupt, Mark Wahlberg supported the proper use of AI in film production and mentioned plans to apply AI technology to stadium background rendering in his upcoming Pete Rose biopic. He opposed using ChatGPT to write screenplays or replace real actors, emphasizing that the essence of film should reside in authentic storytelling rather than technology-driven special effects. He responded positively to the idea of his children or grandchildren managing his AI likeness rights after his death to cast him in quality films, or having the 'Ted' animated series continue for decades through AI replication. Veritas expands its anti-counterfeit chip to jewelry, trophies, and fragrance bottles Startup Veritas is expanding the application of its anti-counterfeit verification chip to jewelry, trophies, fragrance bottles, and more to combat sophisticated fakes. The proliferation of 'superfakes' that meticulously replicate brand markings is on the rise. Startup Veritas is tackling the issue by developing proprietary verification chip technology capable of authenticating genuine products. The company is expanding its anti-counterfeit chips from existing applications into diverse product categories such as jewelry, trophies, and fragrance bottles. Replit’s CEO says the future of work is the ‘self-driving company’ Amjad Masad, CEO of Replit, presented a vision for the future of work at TechCrunch Disrupt, describing the 'self-driving company' where AI agents handle repetitive tasks while humans focus on judgment and planning. The shift in the work environment is accelerating as AI agents take full charge of repetitive baseline tasks, such as analyzing support tickets and applying code patches, while humans focus on critical judgment and decision-making. Rather than replacing human jobs, AI transforms the way work is done, allowing employees to dedicate more time to architectural design, strategic planning, and creative discussions. Although most people have not yet felt a drastic shift in their jobs due to AI, the adoption of AI agents is expected to drive positive changes, boosting both employee productivity and job satisfaction. A week with RTX Spark: everything we’ve learned using Nvidia’s new laptops A one-week hands-on review and performance validation of the ultra-premium laptop lineup powered by Nvidia's high-performance Arm-based 'RTX Spark N1X' superchip. The RTX Spark N1X features a Blackwell architecture GPU (6,144 CUDA cores), 20 CPU cores, and up to 128GB of unified memory, supporting local large AI model execution and demanding tasks. It is featured in flagship devices from manufacturers such as HP, Asus, Lenovo, and Microsoft, launching as an ultra-high-end lineup priced from $2,599.99 up to $6,999.99. Thanks to expanded native Arm app support and improvements in Prism emulation, the Windows on Arm experience has stabilized significantly, aside from x86-only specialized software or select game compatibility issues. Meta’s new terms may allow it to train AI on news sites for free Concerns are being raised that Meta's new business terms may allow the company to use content from corporate websites, including news publishers utilizing its platform, for AI training free of charge. Under Meta's new commercial terms effective October 30th, content from websites linked to business accounts using its platforms, such as Facebook and Instagram, can be collected and used for AI development, improvement, research, and training. Legal experts pointed out that these terms could take precedence over website robots.txt blocking settings, potentially leading to free AI training without formal licensing agreements; however, a Meta spokesperson countered that there is no change to how crawlers are used and that robots.txt settings continue to be respected. Amid Meta's recent introduction of paid subscriptions with link-sharing features (Meta One) for business and creator accounts, criticism has also emerged that Meta will be allowed to crawl the very same content for free. Liquid AI Releases d1-ultra: A Multimodal Decision Model That Scores 65 on the AA Decision Index Liquid AI has launched d1-ultra, a multimodal decision model that takes text and image inputs and returns probability scores for structured queries via a single forward pass without generating output tokens. d1-ultra processes text and images simultaneously with zero output token generation, enabling cost-effective routing, verification, guardrails, and replacement of LLM-as-a-judge. It is exclusively available via the Liquid API at $0.042 per million input tokens, with no downloadable weights released. According to Liquid, it led across all 112 internal evaluation categories and ranked #1 on the Artificial Analysis Decision Index with a score of 65. Meta Releases Muse Spark Open: 816B Open-Weight MoE With 1M Context Meta has unveiled 'Muse Spark Open,' a native multimodal open-weight MoE model featuring a 1M-token context window and 816B total parameters. It features an MoE architecture with 816B total parameters, where 42B are active per token (8 out of 256 experts), supporting a 1M-token context window and native multimodal input. While demonstrating strong performance in agent tool use (MCP-Atlas 89.2) and knowledge reliability metrics, it slightly trailed leading open models such as Kimi K3 and GLM 5.3 in coding (Terminal-Bench 2.1 76.4). In addition to the BF16 weights (approximately 1.6 TB), an expert-quantized NVFP4 build (approximately 530 GB) is provided, enabling execution in environments with two Apple M5 Ultra Mac Studios or four Blackwell Ultra GPUs. Agentic RAG vs. Naive RAG: When Your Retrieval System Should Think for Itself This article compares and analyzes the architecture, pros and cons, and adoption timing of Agentic RAG—which performs autonomous reasoning and iterative retrieval to overcome the limitations of simple, single-retrieval Naive RAG. Naive RAG follows a linear pipeline of 'retrieve → read → generate,' making it suitable for simple queries, but it shows limitations in complex queries that require multi-step reasoning or multi-source synthesis. Agentic RAG treats retrieval as a tool for reasoning rather than a fixed single step, deriving optimal answers by evaluating retrieval results and conducting iterative searches. It provides decision criteria for selecting the appropriate RAG architecture based on workflow complexity and query characteristics in enterprise applications. 5 Best Resources to Learn About World Models in 2026 Introduces the concept of world models emerging as a core next-generation AI technology, along with recommended lectures and research materials for learning them as of 2026. World models are an AI technology that goes beyond simple input-output mapping to learn how environments operate and simulate the future to plan actions. Recommended foundational learning resources include UC Berkeley's CS 285 course covering reinforcement learning and model-based planning, as well as Stanford's CS348K course covering spatial computing and latent representations. Highlights seminal world model research by David Ha and Jürgen Schmidhuber as core material, explaining learning methods within simulated environments through three key components: vision model, memory model, and controller. The World Won't Wait: Evaluating and Teaching Agents Under a Live Clock This research introduces a benchmark for evaluating LLM agents in dynamic environments that evolve in real time during inference, along with a two-timescale self-improvement framework designed to address latency issues. Proposed RealtimeBench, consisting of 180 tasks across 6 game types where games continue running without pausing, to evaluate challenges arising from inference and tool-calling latencies. Confirmed the impact of latency on performance: GPT-6.1-sol, which excels in paused-game environments, fell behind the faster Jev model in real-time settings. Proposed the TORSI framework, where a slower teacher model analyzes failures offline to generate reusable guidelines, and a faster student model executes real-time gameplay based on them. Counterfactual Neighborhood Risk Fields: Evidence-Grounded Selective Safety for Vision-Language Models This study proposes CNRF, a framework that selectively controls safety by disentangling toxicity from familiarity based on counterfactual evidence, rather than relying on blanket refusals in vision-language models (VLMs). To overcome the limitations of existing representation-based safety defenses, it introduces the Counterfactual Neighborhood Risk Fields (CNRF) framework, which constructs contextually matched counterfactual pairs and disentangles the measurement of risk from neighborhood support. By preventing unfamiliar inputs from being misclassified as harmful, it enables selective intervention and constraint-guided generation of alternative responses instead of outright refusals. Across three VLMs and five multimodal jailbreak attack families, it achieved a macro defense success rate of 86.06-89.30% while preserving utility on benign tasks. IneqAgent: Agentic Inequality Proving in Lean with Symbolic Computation This research introduces IneqAgent, an agentic framework that automatically proves mathematical inequalities within the Lean4 environment by combining the reasoning capabilities of large language models with symbolic computation. It seamlessly orchestrates lemma construction, goal reformulation, and symbolic computation backend selection via lemma-level Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS). It utilizes complementary backends—such as sum-of-squares (SOS), Bernstein polynomials, and successive difference-substitution—to generate local proof certificates and integrate them with LLM reasoning steps. Using DeepSeek-V4-Flash as the base model, it achieved the highest success rate by solving 175 out of 186 problems (94.09%), and verified inequalities related to the Dittert and Merris conjectures in Lean. L2G-SR: Learning to Guide LLM-based Symbolic Regression This research introduces L2G-SR, a framework that guides the search process of large language model-based symbolic regression using a learned structural verifier. To overcome the limitation of conventional symbolic regression—which relies solely on numerical errors and often selects inaccurate proxy models rather than the correct mathematical formula structure—a structural verifier based on Tree Edit Distance (TED) was introduced. Even without the target formula available during search, the verifier complements numerical error to select parent candidates, proposing refined formulas through analyzer and generator modules. Benchmark evaluations on LLM-SRBench demonstrated state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance across multiple LLM backbones and domains in terms of numerical fit, formula-level accuracy, and TED metrics. When Reality Outpaces Cognition: A New Scaling Principle for Reliable Intelligence This study proposes a new scaling principle and benchmark addressing the limits within which intelligent systems can reliably perceive, reason, and act amidst rapid real-world changes. Established the concept of Effective Coherent Cognition Speed (S_C^eff), quantifying the maximum speed of reality change at which an intelligent system can reliably execute actions as the gap between observation and reality widens. Evaluated reinforcement learning agents trained at a baseline speed by increasing the speed of reality up to 50x without retraining, while keeping computational resources fixed. Confirmed that the Co^4 architecture, inspired by cellular mechanisms of conscious information processing and wakeful thought, achieves a significantly higher Effective Coherent Cognition Speed compared to Transformer and gated-MLP baseline models. Feature Elicitation for SAE Steering of Deceptive Behaviour in Small LLMs This research applies Sparse Autoencoder (SAE) steering techniques to mechanistically mitigate alignment faking and compliance gaps in small large language models (LLMs), identifying novel deceptive behaviors. By using a novel bag-of-words feature elicitation pipeline to steer deontological ethics-related SAE features, the compliance gap of Gemma-3-27B-IT was reduced to a statistically insignificant level. The identically tested setup was not effective in mitigating the compliance gap in the LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct model. Through scratchpad trace analysis, the study newly identified 'deceptive defanging' behavior, where the model appears compliant on the surface while intentionally degrading functionality. Same Answer, New Box: How Much of RLVR's Out-of-Domain Gain Is Answer Format? This study reveals that a significant portion of the out-of-domain performance gains observed in reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) stems from formatting answers to suit answer extractors rather than genuine improvements in reasoning capabilities. It demonstrated that much of the performance improvement shown by RLVR models trained on math data across other domains such as MMLU results from placing answers in formats easily read by extractors (e.g., \\boxed{}). Evaluation of four publicly available pairs of 7B SimpleRL models revealed that 82% of the out-of-domain multiple-choice gains reported by existing extractors (77% according to independent LLM evaluation) were an illusion caused by format matching. In contrast, virtually no format gains were observed in 32B model pairs trained with different recipes, proving that reported performance improvements heavily depend not only on the models but also on the characteristics of the extractors used. KNOU-Flood: Advancing global streamflow forecasting with lead-aware probabilistic machine learning The KNOU-Flood study enhances global streamflow and flood forecasting accuracy as well as uncertainty estimation by refining the existing single-layer LSTM-based hydrological forecasting recipe through lead-time-aware inputs, GraphCast weather forecast data, and latent noise ensembling. While maintaining the recurrent neural network's core architecture (single-layer LSTM), the study introduces an extended 424-day input window, lead-time-aware forecast inputs, multi-weather forecast integration from ECMWF HRES and GraphCast, and a fair CRPS-based latent noise ensemble technique. In evaluations across 2,099 test basins from 2021 to 2023, KNOU-Flood achieved a median KGE' of 0.65 at lead time 1 day (LT1) and 0.48 at 10 days (LT10), showing significant performance improvements across all lead times compared to the baseline AIFL recipe (0.62 and 0.34). The study revealed that the steep performance drop of existing models with increasing lead times was largely driven by the forecast input protocol, and significantly improved 1.5-year and 2-year return period flood detection performance over baseline models via probabilistic ensembling. Causal Syntax Circuits in CodeLMs: Budget and Probe Decide What Looks Localised This study analyzes the default Python bias behavior of Code Language Models (CodeLMs) from a mechanistic perspective, establishing that claims of specific behaviors being localized within models heavily depend on analysis configurations such as exploration budgets and probe designs. Code models' default preference for Python increases with model size, and instruction-tuned models exhibited an average of 12% higher preference than base models. Bias measurements fluctuated significantly depending on the evaluation probe method, with measurements for the same model dropping sharply from approximately 1.6 to 0.01 when prompts were replaced with XLCoST parallel functions. Whether behavior appears as a localized circuit depends on component budget settings (e.g., 1 out of 12 budget vs. 6 out of 40), making transparent disclosure of analysis conditions essential when claiming circuit localization. Learning Topology-Aware Representations for Mechanics-Relevant TPMS Metamaterials This study explores a topology-aware representation learning framework that leverages persistent homology to learn the global structural connectivity of Triply Periodic Minimal Surface (TPMS) metamaterials. Proposed a framework integrating persistent homology into a DMAE backbone to explicitly encode global structural connectivity directly linked to mechanical behavior. Introduced a persistent topological feature-guided masking technique alongside an auxiliary decoder designed to preserve the counts of topological features such as connected components, tunnels, and cavities. Evaluated on two TPMS datasets, demonstrating up to a 3.9% improvement in family classification accuracy and significantly enhancing unsupervised property clustering performance by up to 51.5%. DeepUnfolding_Agent: A Classical_Quantum Adaptive Control API This study proposes DeepUnfolding_Agent, a schema-based FastAPI control platform that decouples controller logic into an external service across heterogeneous environments such as machine learning and quantum optimization to enhance reusability and safety. It decouples and provides controller logic—which was previously tightly coupled to training scripts and limited reusability and safety verification—as an external software service. It isolates controller intelligence from domain execution through an architecture where external executors register strategy schemas, submit diagnostic variables, receive schema-constrained control actions, and return post-execution feedback. It supports execution paths including classical, hybrid, Qiskit Aer, and real IBM Quantum hardware, and validates adaptive control performance across diverse workflows such as finance, healthcare, and genomics. PRISM: PRIvileged Sibling-guided Multi-Agent Topology Design This paper proposes PRISM, a novel learning framework that leverages privileged information to design multi-agent collaboration topologies optimized for task queries. In LLM-based multi-agent systems, generating collaboration topologies that determine how agents participate and communicate has emerged as a key challenge. Existing supervised fine-tuning (SFT) methods have the limitation of being biased toward frequently appearing topologies due to the lack of preference ranking among graphs in the corpus. PRISM guides query-specific optimal topology design by having a teacher model reference sibling graphs—which are unavailable at deployment time—as privileged information, and training a student model to imitate it. A Large-Scale Dataset for Multi-Class Financial News Classification in Brazilian Portuguese Presents a web-scraped dataset of approximately 160,000 financial news articles and benchmark experimental results for similarity-based multi-class classification to advance Brazilian Portuguese financial natural language processing (NLP) research. Constructed a large-scale corpus of approximately 160,000 financial news articles to address the data scarcity issue in the Brazilian Portuguese financial domain. Built distinct sub-datasets using manual labeling and Large Language Model (LLM) annotations for a semantic similarity-based weakly supervised benchmark, evaluating Cosine Similarity, KNN, and FAISS. While KNN achieved the highest performance on the manually labeled dataset (accuracy 47.3%, F1-score 31.9%), the F1-score plummeted to around 20% on the LLM-annotated dataset due to noise and class imbalance, underscoring the vital importance of high-quality manual labeling. Real-time Chatbot Romance Scam Detection in Stage Classification This research presents a chatbot system that detects romance scams in real time on messaging platforms and offers preventive advice, leveraging large language models and social science's seven stage theories. Developed a chatbot system integrating the Line Messaging API to detect romance scam conversations on social media in real time. Combines social science's 'seven stage theories' with LLMs to precisely classify the progression stages of scams. Upon detecting a scam, it immediately provides users with explanations of the current scam stage and tailored prevention guidelines to mitigate the risk of harm. MeddeX: A Retrieval-Augmented LLaMA-3 System with Gemini Vision for Evidence-Backed Medical Report Analysis This paper proposes MeddeX, a medical report analysis system that combines QLoRA fine-tuned LLaMA-3, Gemini Vision, and RAG to perform accurate, evidence-backed analysis of complex medical reports. Built a hybrid extraction pipeline capable of processing text-based PDFs, scanned image PDFs, and radiological images, along with a corrupted text detection mechanism Introduced report scoping retrieval, an automated longitudinal comparison engine, and an on-demand self-healing backfill pipeline, moving beyond single-document question answering Demonstrated performance improvements across key metrics such as ROUGE and BLEU compared to the baseline LLaMA3-Med42-8B model, confirming its potential for practical clinical decision support Scaling Indic Pretraining: 2.8 Trillion Tokens and an Open Data Generation Pipeline NVIDIA and IIT Madras's AI4Bharat research lab have released a pretraining dataset of approximately 2.8 trillion tokens supporting 22 official Indian languages, along with an open-source data generation pipeline. NVIDIA and AI4Bharat announced the 'Nemotron-pretraining-Indic-v1' corpus, spanning approximately 2.8 trillion tokens across 22 Indian languages, alongside an open-source GPU-accelerated data generation pipeline. Providing a total of 34 language-script configurations covering native scripts for 22 languages and Romanized text for 12 languages, it applies document translation, quality evaluation, and restoration workflows that preserve formulas, tables, and code. The full data generation recipe—including prompts, LLM workflows, and quality verification—is open-sourced to enable developers to directly scale and build large-scale synthetic data for Indian languages using their own sources. Welcome Beam: a 501B open model you can own, customize, and scale Reflection, an emerging US-based AI research lab, has released 'Beam' on Hugging Face—a 501B open-source MoE language model specialized for coding and agentic workflows. Beam adopts an MoE architecture with 501B total parameters where 23B are activated per token, supporting a long context window of 1M tokens under an Apache 2.0 license. In addition to its proprietary Rust-based harness, it officially supports major inference frameworks such as Transformers, vLLM, and SGLang from day one, while offering recipes for supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement learning (RL). It features various quantization formats (BF16, FP8, NVFP4), 5-level reasoning intensity adjustment, multi-turn reasoning retention (preserve_thinking), and dedicated grammar-based tool calling. Reverse Engineering Hardware with AI Introduces a methodology and case study for rapidly reverse engineering and developing Linux drivers for undocumented hardware using AI agents and virtual machine-based traffic capture. Secured normal operation reference data by passing through the device to a KVM Windows virtual machine and capturing traffic with usbmon on the host. Reduced development time from months to hours by letting AI handle repetitive reverse engineering tasks such as analyzing thousands of lines of hex dumps, inferring byte meanings, and writing tests. Released the hw-harness tool generalizing these workflows, and applied it to scanner drivers and laptop MIPI camera image quality improvements. Expert Analysis Warns Meta's New Commercial Terms Could Permit Scraping of News Sites Meta has updated its commercial terms to allow the collection of content from websites linked to business accounts for AI training and other purposes, sparking backlash and concern among businesses, including news publishers. Meta revised its commercial terms to allow crawling of domains and web content associated with business accounts to train AI models and improve products. Opting out of the terms is not possible, meaning those who do not agree must completely cease commercial use of Meta business products such as Facebook and Instagram. Legal experts warned that these terms could take precedence over robots.txt blocks, potentially being exploited as justification for unauthorized training on content worth millions of dollars. Marketing Engineer: Running 100x More Experiments with AI Agents Explains the concept and role of a 'Marketing Engineer' who significantly reduces marketing execution costs and builds a continuous large-scale experimentation system by combining AI agents and coding tools. By bridging the separation between planning and implementation that was previously a bottleneck in marketing execution, it is now possible for a single person to build a system that directly conducts dozens of marketing experiments per day. The human role shifts from simple bulk content production to idea curation, final approval, injecting business context, and continuous system improvement. Using tools such as Claude Code, Cursor, and MCP, practitioners integrate with CRM and revenue data, directly implementing automated pipelines and approval workflows in code. A 3-Stage Strategy to Scale Monthly Social Views from 0 to 100 Million Introduces a 3-stage social media growth strategy for early-stage startups to scale monthly social views from 0 to 100 million step-by-step, focusing on acquiring customers genuinely interested in the product rather than meaningless virality. In the early stage (0 to 1 million monthly views), avoid viral content irrelevant to the product and strictly filter and acquire a real potential customer base through founder-led storytelling and daily posting. In the growth stage (1 million to 10 million monthly views), heavily invest in scaling content across proven channels by bringing in dedicated talent such as copywriters and short-form video creators. In the scaling stage (10 million to 100 million monthly views), transition from the founder's personal influence to the overall brand and build dedicated teams per channel to distribute 20+ pieces of content per day across all channels. Why on Earth Are We Doing All This? A satirical piece on contemporary scientific research culture, where mass-generated, uncertain AI outputs and the accumulation of publication metrics overwhelm deep human understanding and the joy of inquiry. Companies such as OpenAI are mass-producing probabilistically plausible research documents, shifting the difficult burden of verification and comprehension onto academia. It criticizes the situation by drawing an analogy to Borges's 'Library of Babel', emphasizing that even if correct answers exist, they are meaningless if one cannot comprehend or filter them. Focusing solely on paper volume or result production risks dismissing the core values of research—such as curiosity, creativity, and lucid explanation—as inefficiencies, reducing researchers to mere AI approvers. How many of your paper (either paper you authored or reviewed) are desk rejected? [D] This post discusses the desk rejection status of papers submitted and reviewed at the ICLR conference, as well as suspicions regarding bypassing the submission limit using multiple accounts. The author was notified in the morning that a paper they had reviewed for 4 hours while sick and given a 3/4 score was desk rejected. Many papers around the author are also being observed getting desk rejected. A rumor was shared that someone used multiple accounts to bypass the 20-paper submission limit, resulting in all of their papers being desk rejected. To reviewers: do you upload your review to an AI? (NOT the paper itself) [D] A post discussing the practice and experience of paper reviewers uploading their own written reviews to an AI for feedback, rather than the original manuscript. The author does not upload the original paper to protect confidentiality and author rights, but instead uploads only their own drafted review to the AI to request a second look. Although the additional review process increases the workload, they experienced higher-quality reviews and fewer typos in most cases. The author asks the community whether other academic reviewers are similarly leveraging AI to improve the quality of their reviews. The Surface Laptop Ultra delivers on most of Microsoft's promises A review article reporting that Microsoft's new Surface Laptop Ultra delivers on its overall promises by simultaneously offering high-performance local AI computing and high-end gaming capabilities. Equipped with Nvidia's new RTX Spark platform, the Surface Laptop Ultra supports running 70-billion (70B) parameter large AI models locally and playing AAA games. Microsoft presents an all-in-one solution targeting prospective MacBook Pro customers and high-performance users who previously purchased separate gaming desktops and work laptops. It is a notable new product that emerged amid a stagnant PC market marked by a 20% year-over-year decline in global PC shipments in the third quarter of 2026 and soaring RAM prices ('RAMpocalypse'). Rocket Report: FAA eyes more Starbase traffic; India's Skyroot snares US customer Covers global space launch news, including the scheduled launch of Japan's Martian moon probe and US startup Reflect Orbital's launch contract with India's Skyroot. Japan's Martian Moons eXploration (MMX) mission is scheduled to launch on a Japanese H3 rocket on Monday, US time. The MMX probe plans to explore Mars' moons Phobos and Deimos, deploy a rover, and return samples to Earth in 2031. Space mirror technology startup Reflect Orbital signed a contract with India's Skyroot Aerospace for two launches aboard Vikram-1 and upgraded Vikram-1U rockets. How many jobs is your state losing to AI? This dashboard tracks the cuts – and the cost The Center for AI Safety (CAIS) has released a dashboard tracking the scale of AI-driven job cuts in the United States and the resulting household wage losses by state. The Center for AI Safety's (CAIS) 'Wage Loss Dashboard' tracks AI-induced job cuts and wage losses based on layoff data starting from January 2026. According to the dashboard, more than 117,000 jobs nationwide have been cut due to AI this year, resulting in an estimated annual wage loss of approximately $8.5 billion. Translated to a national average, this amounts to an annual wage loss effect of about $25 per person, with California—where layoffs are concentrated—reaching a per-person loss of $56. AI in Production: Panellists Highlight Silent Failures, Harness Engineering, and Agent Governance Practicing engineering experts discussed silent failures, code review bottlenecks, governance, and the need for continuous evaluation infrastructure when deploying agentic systems. Rather than crashing abruptly, AI produces plausible errors, leading to silent failures—such as 'semantic drift' that distorts domain concepts or 'embedding drift' triggered by embedding model updates—where the system malfunctions even while infrastructure status checks report normal operation. The soaring volume of code generated by coding agents has pushed conventional line-by-line manual code reviews to their limits, necessitating that syntax checks be automated via CI/CD while humans focus on reviewing high-level architecture and business intent. Agent evaluation must be treated as continuous operational infrastructure rather than a one-time pre-release checklist, incorporating span-level online tracing and mutation testing. Grafana Applies SLOs and Error Budgets to AI Agent Behaviour Grafana Labs has proposed adopting traditional SRE concepts of SLOs and error budgets to measure and manage complex behavioral qualities of AI agents, such as hallucinations, toxicity, and task completion rates. Moving beyond the limitations of traditional technical metrics like latency and token consumption, it converts the actual behavioral quality of agents—such as hallucination occurrences and task completion—into SLI metrics for evaluation. Rather than relying on a single composite quality score, it measures dimensions like hallucinations, cost, and toxicity individually to prevent failure causes from being masked by aggregate metrics. Based on defined target thresholds and the allowable margin of error (error budgets), it supports a decision-making model that accelerates experimentation and development when budget remains, while prioritizing quality improvements when the budget is depleted. Mini book: Evolutionary Architecture in Practice This mini-book covers practical strategies and case studies for evolutionary architecture to safely and sustainably evolve systems amid AI adoption and rapid technological changes. Presents approaches for utilizing Agentic Fitness Functions that go beyond deterministic rules to evaluate semantic drift and architectural risks within CI/CD pipelines Emphasizes the need to treat system comprehension as an essential architectural characteristic and prevent cognitive debt as AI-generated code increases Introduces real-world case-based architectural evolution frameworks, including scaling from B2C to B2B, ensuring multi-cloud/region autonomy, and transitioning AI prototypes into distributed systems Presentation: Exploding GPUs This presentation explains why the security of AI workloads in cloud-native environments is inherently tied to Kubernetes security, and covers related threats and defense strategies. AI infrastructure security is fundamentally rooted in Kubernetes security, with non-deterministic agent behavior and shared computing environments acting as new threats. It explores the evolving AI threat landscape, including GPU supply chain vulnerabilities and the risks associated with cost and resource management in large-scale GPU clusters. It presents practical defense strategies to protect production cloud-native AI workloads, ranging from building prompt firewalls to managing agent identities. AWS Introduces Strands Decider 2B and Strands Box for AI Agent Development AWS has unveiled 'Strands Decider 2B', a compact open-source model designed to assist AI agent decision-making, and 'Strands Box', a sandbox for secure execution control. Strands Decider 2B is a 2-billion-parameter compact model designed not for generating long texts, but for making optimal decisions among predefined choices such as tool selection, model routing, and guardrails. Built upon Qwen3.5-2B with pointer heads and LoRA applied, its weights and training data are openly available, enabling direct modification and execution in local environments. Strands Box is a developer preview sandbox combining OS-level isolation with the open-source policy language Dogwood to securely control agent shell access, Python execution, network access, and more. AI Decision Models and Their Impact on AI SOC Platforms AI decision models that bypass text generation and return probability-based, structured selections are revolutionizing alert triage efficiency and auditability in Security Operations Centers (SOC). Recently, decision models such as TypeSafe AI's Jev and OpenAI's Decisions API have been emerging, which skip text generation and directly return choices, scores, and probabilities for given states and questions. Decision models are optimal for alert triage tasks by providing benefits such as automation potential via threshold setting, extremely low costs (around $42 per billion tokens), and easy audit trails through a fixed answer space. The fact that a model does not hallucinate merely means that it adheres to a defined schema; since it can still produce incorrect answers, input data quality and the context of model calibration must be taken into account. China-Linked Hackers Exploited BeyondTrust Zero-Day to Take Over Remote Support Servers Covers an attack case where China-linked threat group Armory Castle exploited a zero-day vulnerability (CVE-2026-102764) in BeyondTrust Remote Support to take over remote support servers and distribute malicious webshells. China-linked cyber espionage group Armory Castle exploited an origin validation vulnerability in the internal messaging component of BeyondTrust Remote Support (CVE-2026-102764, CVSS 9.3) as a zero-day. BeyondTrust completed deploying patches for SaaS customers on October 9, 2026, and recommended self-hosted customers running versions 24.3.1 through 26.2.3 to apply the BT26-05-RS patch. Following successful compromise, the attackers installed the SAWFLY lightweight PHP webshell, disguised and copied the system shell (/bin/sh) to gain root privileges, and attempted lateral movement within the internal network. MS Unveils 2.8 Trillion Parameter Model Ahead of Official Launch, Highlighting In-House Capabilities to Reduce Reliance on OpenAI Microsoft announced the completion of pre-training for 'MAI-Base-2', an in-house large-scale AI model with 2.8 trillion parameters, aiming to reduce its reliance on external models. Microsoft completed pre-training for 'MAI-Base-2', an MoE-based model featuring 80 billion active parameters out of 2.8 trillion total parameters. Trained over 38 days using 16,000 Nvidia GB200 GPUs, it recorded a 4.2x to 12.3x compute efficiency improvement compared to DeepSeek V4 Pro Base. Microsoft plans to prioritize deploying this model across its own products and supply weights to enterprise customers and trusted partners via its Foundry platform. Google Co-Founder: \"AI Participates in Developing Successor Models Starting from Flash... Entering the RSI Trajectory\" Google co-founder Sergey Brin stated that AI directly participates in the development of Gemini models, entering the stage of recursive self-improvement (RSI). Google has entered the trajectory of 'recursive self-improvement (RSI),' accelerating development speed and performance enhancements by deploying existing AI models to develop successor Gemini models. AI agents assist by automating significant portions of R&D and production service code, including training monitoring, bug detection, and code generation. The new model 'Gemini 4 Argon' features improved writing styles through direct editing and feedback by authors, as well as an expanded token limit for complex reasoning. Twelve Labs Launches 'Jockey', an AI Agent That Finds Scenes and Edits Videos Upon Verbal Request Twelve Labs has launched 'Jockey', a video understanding AI agent capable of searching for scenes within videos and editing them using natural language commands. Performs video search, rough highlight editing, and clip extraction based on user speech, powered by proprietary foundation models along with embedding and metadata generation technologies Supports reference video recommendations through trend analysis when YouTube and TikTok links are provided, as well as team sharing capabilities Expected to reduce marketing video production time and costs by integrating with major AI applications like ChatGPT and Claude, and supporting custom service integration via API OpenAI Unveils Voice Model 'GPT-Live-1.1'... Voice Improvements for 6 Languages Including Korean OpenAI has unveiled the voice model 'GPT-Live-1.1', which significantly improves pronunciation and intonation across six languages including Korean and reduces latency. Addition of new voices and improvements to natural intonations for Korean, French, German, Hindi, Mexican Spanish, and Japanese Addition of new English voice 'Bubbie' and reduced waiting times by utilizing diverse phrases instead of repetitive prompts during information retrieval and waiting periods Application of GPT-Live-1.1 and the lightweight version 'GPT-Live-1.1 Quip' to ChatGPT, Codex, and dots, with developer APIs to be provided at a later date \"More Natural Korean Accent\"... OpenAI Updates 'ChatGPT Voice' OpenAI has updated ChatGPT's voice conversation feature, 'Voice', to improve Korean accents, add new voices, and enhance the natural flow of conversation. Significantly improved multilingual accents and vocal expression, including Korean, through the 'GPT-Live-1.1' model Added two new Korean voices: male voice 'Hanul' and female voice 'Haessal' Applied a full-duplex architecture to seamlessly continue conversations even during interruptions or pauses Rolled out sequentially to both free and paid ChatGPT users worldwide, including South Korea Cohere Pursues Acquisition of AI Security Startup AISLE Canadian AI company Cohere is pursuing the acquisition of AISLE, a startup specializing in AI-based detection and remediation of software security vulnerabilities. Cohere and AISLE signed a letter of intent (LOI) to pursue the acquisition, aiming to combine autonomous security systems with the development of advanced AI models. AISLE holds a track record of discovering over 500 new CVE vulnerabilities across more than 100 software products in about a year. Upon completion of the acquisition, a new office in Prague, Czech Republic, and a new security organization named 'Cohere Cyber' will be established, with AISLE's key executives joining Cohere's security leadership. Fibocom Showcases Next-Generation Connectivity and AIoT Portfolio at KES 2026 At the Korea Electronics Show (KES 2026), Fibocom unveiled its next-generation AIoT portfolio, including the latest 5G broadband and AI companion solutions, and announced achievements from its domestic partnerships. Unveiled global partnership achievements, including equipping Humax Networks' FWA products with the 5G module (FG550) for mass production targeting Southeast Asian telecom operators Completed KC certification and KT network interworking approval for the LTE Cat 1 bis module (LE270-KR), supplying it for mass production in Korean smart metering projects Demonstrated an expanded portfolio transitioning from wireless connectivity to device intelligence, featuring the AI companion solution MagiCore, smart modules, and 5G RedCap Permissions Given to AI Lead to Disaster: Entire Show Evaporates Right Before Broadcast The risks of AI agent access permissions to servers and data have come under the spotlight after the entire final cut of a Swedish reality show was deleted, forcing the broadcast to be postponed. The final edited broadcast cut of the major Swedish reality show 'The Challenge Sverige' was deleted, postponing the premiere date from November 22 to 2027. While initially suspected to be the doing of an AI agent, the broadcaster placed greater weight on a backup failure and human error rather than a standalone AI glitch. Following a series of database and file deletion incidents involving AI agents like Replit and GPT-5.6 Sol, the need for caution and proper backups when granting server permissions has been underscored. Intel and AMD Compete in Rack-Scale High-Density CPUs Targeting Agentic AI In response to the proliferation of agentic AI, Intel and AMD have unveiled high-density rack-scale infrastructure designs and entered into competition to meet the growing demand for CPU computing in data centers. The importance of CPUs in data centers is surging rapidly due to the expansion of complex workloads such as multi-AI agent orchestration, task management, and tool calling. At the OCP Summit, Intel announced a design integrating over 46,000 cores per rack based on Xeon 6+, while AMD announced the 'Helion' rack design supporting up to 32,768 cores based on 6th Gen EPYC. Both companies project that the CPU-to-GPU ratio in data centers will approach 1:1 or that CPUs could surpass GPUs, and they are targeting the market with OCP-based open rack standards."